text-[#0D0D0D] dark:text-[#F3F3F3] "><div>the scheme is the ed25519 digital signature. my improvement is that if i multiply the public key’s generator point G0 with a new generator point G1  and use k<em>G1</em>G0 as the new public key, then any signature maker can derive the new signature offline, with no communication with the signature checker, if G1, G2, G3 etc. are a known series from a hash or just any pre-agreement. my question is if it is still safe to have a k*G0 multiplied by publicly known numbers, and derive new private and public keypairs that way?</div>
